Draft for discussion. This document frames the problem and presents the candidate activation mechanisms. Options A, B and C were written before a fourth was built; that fourth, the base-version supermajority latch, is implemented and tested and is the one this draft now recommends. The other three are retained because the discussion refers to them by letter and because the tradeoffs that ruled them out are the argument for the fourth. Nothing here should be read as a settled decision.

== Abstract ==

This DIP proposes adding Segregated Witness (SegWit) to Dogecoin by deriving from BIP141 (segwit), BIP143 (signature verification), BIP144 (peer services), BIP147 (NULLDUMMY), and BIP173 (bech32 addresses), with the Dogecoin-specific modifications required to make activation possible on a chain whose block <code>nVersion</code> field is already used by AuxPoW.

The consensus-validation, script-interpreter, serialization, and weight machinery for SegWit are already present in Dogecoin Core, inherited from Bitcoin Core, but have never been activated. The bech32/witness address layer and <code>getnewaddress</code> wallet support are not inherited; they are implemented in the branches cited below and verified on regtest. The single unresolved blocker is activation signaling: the AuxPoW chain ID occupies the same high bits of <code>nVersion</code> that BIP9 versionbits requires, so standard BIP9 miner signaling cannot work as-is. This DIP documents that collision and presents four candidate activation mechanisms. The fourth, a base-version supermajority latch that keeps signalling inside <code>nVersion</code> but reads only the low base-version byte, sidesteps the collision without new signalling machinery and is implemented and tested on regtest; it is the recommended resolution.

== Motivation ==

SegWit provides transaction malleability fixes, a foundation for second-layer protocols (payment channels, which prior DIP drafts on CSV and channel primitives depend on), witness-discounted block capacity, and native bech32 addresses. Dogecoin already carries the dormant consensus code; the question is not whether the feature can be built but how it can be safely turned on given Dogecoin's merge-mining architecture.

This proposal exists because the obvious path, "do what Bitcoin did, BIP9 versionbits on bit 1", is not available to Dogecoin without modification, and the reason is subtle enough that it deserves a written, reviewable explanation before any activation parameters are debated.

== The core problem: AuxPoW / versionbits collision ==

Dogecoin reuses the 32-bit block-header <code>nVersion</code> field for two unrelated purposes that overlap in the high bits.

AuxPoW chain ID encoding (<code>src/primitives/pureheader.h</code>):

<source lang="cpp">
static const int32_t VERSION_AUXPOW      = (1 << 8);   // bit 8: auxpow flag
static const int32_t VERSION_CHAIN_START = (1 << 16);  // bits 16+: chain ID

int32_t GetChainId() const   { return nVersion >> 16; }
void    SetBaseVersion(int32_t nBaseVersion, int32_t nChainId) {
    nVersion = nBaseVersion | (nChainId * VERSION_CHAIN_START);
}
int32_t GetBaseVersion() const { return nVersion % VERSION_AUXPOW; } // low 8 bits
</source>

Layout of a merge-mined Dogecoin block's <code>nVersion</code>:

<pre>
  bit 31 ............ 16 | 15 ...... 9 | 8 | 7 ........ 0
  +---------------------+-------------+---+-------------+
  |   AuxPoW chain ID   |  reserved   |aux|  base ver   |
  +---------------------+-------------+---+-------------+
</pre>

The mainnet/testnet chain ID is <code>0x0062</code> (<code>consensus.nAuxpowChainId</code>). Encoded into the header that is <code>0x0062 << 16 = 0x00620000</code>.

BIP9 versionbits (<code>src/versionbits.h</code>) requires the opposite:

<source lang="cpp">
static const int32_t VERSIONBITS_TOP_BITS = 0x20000000UL; // top 3 bits = 0b001
static const int32_t VERSIONBITS_TOP_MASK = 0xE0000000UL;
</source>

A block only counts as a versionbits signal when <code>(nVersion & VERSIONBITS_TOP_MASK) == VERSIONBITS_TOP_BITS</code>: top three bits <code>001</code>, low 29 bits carrying deployment signals.

These are mutually exclusive for a real Dogecoin block:

* Chain ID <code>0x0062</code> sits at bits 16 to 22; the top three bits of <code>0x00620000</code> are <code>000</code>, not <code>001</code>. An AuxPoW-tagged block therefore fails <code>VERSIONBITS_TOP_MASK</code>, so <code>VersionBitsState()</code> never sees it as a signal.
* Conversely, setting <code>0x20000000</code> to signal SegWit (bit 1) would overwrite the chain-ID region, breaking <code>GetChainId()</code> and failing the strict chain-ID check in AuxPoW proof-of-work validation.

There is no <code>nVersion</code> value that is simultaneously a valid AuxPoW header for chain <code>0x0062</code> and a valid BIP9 signal. This is why Dogecoin never shipped SegWit when Bitcoin did, and it is the central decision this DIP raises.

=== Current code state (the safety property) ===

The miner sidesteps the collision by refusing to signal (<code>src/miner.cpp</code>):

<source lang="cpp">
// FIXME: Active version bits after the always-auxpow fork!
// const int32_t nVersion = ComputeBlockVersion(pindexPrev, consensus);
const int32_t nVersion = VERSIONBITS_LAST_OLD_BLOCK_VERSION;   // == 4
pblock->SetBaseVersion(nVersion, nChainId);
</source>

Because the miner emits version <code>4</code> and never sets bit 1, <code>VersionBitsState(... DEPLOYMENT_SEGWIT ...)</code> can never reach <code>LOCKED_IN</code> or <code>ACTIVE</code> on a chain of real blocks, and the witness-commitment enforcement in <code>ContextualCheckBlock</code> (gated on <code>THRESHOLD_ACTIVE</code>) never engages. Setting <code>DEPLOYMENT_SEGWIT.nTimeout</code> to a future date is therefore currently inert: it cannot activate anything while the miner does not signal. This is the property that protects mainnet today, and it is why the activation commit in the reference implementation is marked DO NOT MERGE.

== What is already complete ==

To scope the work correctly: the consensus, script-interpreter, serialization and weight machinery is present in Dogecoin Core (inherited from Bitcoin Core 0.13 to 0.17) and verified by direct read. The address layer is not: master carries no <code>bech32.h</code>, no <code>WitnessV0KeyHash</code> and no <code>address_type</code> on <code>getnewaddress</code>, so that part is carried in the branches below rather than inherited. The consensus blocker is exclusively activation signaling.

